      en: "Anloga is a town of immense historical and cultural significance in the Volta Region of Ghana, serving as the traditional capital of the Anlo-Ewe people. It is situated on a narrow sandbar wedged between the Gulf of Guinea and the expansive Keta Lagoon. This unique geographic setting dictates the local economy, which is centered on fishing and a highly specialized form of agriculture. Anloga is nationally famous for its intensive shallot farming, utilizing sophisticated irrigation techniques on the sandy terrain. Historically, the town was the seat of the Awoamefia (the Paramount Chief) and served as a focal point of resistance against colonial expansion. The Hogbetsotso Festival, held annually in Anloga, commemorates the legendary migration of the Ewe from the tyrannical rule in Notsie (modern-day Togo) and attracts thousands of visitors. The town's architecture reflects a blend of traditional earthen structures and modern designs. Despite facing significant challenges from coastal erosion, Anloga remains a proud symbol of the cultural identity and agricultural ingenuity of the Ewe people.",
